oordinated or escalated","inadequate self-isolation management",""],[1473,577,"Mr Meikle was not subject to ACCT proceedings, despite evidence shortly before death of self-isolation, debt-related vulnerability, known substance misuse and reduced engagement. This","absence of ACCT despite vulnerability indicators",""],[1474,577,"prisoners serving IPP sentences may experience hopelessness, chronic frustration, deterioration in mental health and increased vulnerability to substance misuse and self-neglect","IPP sentence vulnerability",""],[1475,577,"prisoners with known vulnerabilities, substance misuse history and symptoms of deteriorating mental health may not always receive timely psychiatric assessment or sufficiently proactive mental health review","delayed psychiatric input",""],[1476,577,"staff responding to collapse were adequately trained and equipped to consider synthetic cannabinoid intoxication promptly as a possible cause","inadequate emergency response training",""],[1477,577,"staffing pressures, weak supervision, poor staff-prisoner engagement, restricted regimes, and inadequate welfare observations","staffing and regime limitations",""],[1478,577,"concerns about drugs, safety, violence, self-isolation, observation panel compliance, ACCT weaknesses and welfare monitoring at HMP Woodhill had been identified over time","repeated systemic concerns",""],[1479,577,"material information at the eleventh hour","delayed information provision",""],[2336,904,"Mr [name redacted] was the seventh self inflicted death at Woodhill since 2013 and there have been two since. We are concerned that many of the same issues have been repeated in a number of their investigations including this one. In six cases investigated in 2013 and 2014 we found that staff had failed to identify or properly assess the risk of suicide and self harm in newly arrived prisoners.","risk of suicide and self-harm in newly arrived prisoners",""],[2527,972,"The Speech and Language Therapists (SALT) and Dietetic Service had well developed, comprehensive guidelines for investigating and managing patients prone to aspiration","lack of understanding of policies and procedures",""],[2528,972,"both Trusts were working to a degree in a siloed manner","siloed manner",""],[3858,1476,"CNWL failed in its duty of candour in relation to provision of evidence","failure to provide evidence",""],[3859,1476,"SPA call handlers were not sufficiently trained in how to identify patients by using computer searches and by not seeking information appropriately","inadequate training of call handlers",""],[3860,1476,"police officers may need reminding to use the phonetic alphabet and using the word for the month in a person's date of birth","incorrect communication by police officers",""],[3861,1476,"psychiatric liaison nurses and psychiatric liaison doctors should have regard to and specifically consider diagnoses made by other doctors for example those who see such patients repeatedly in A&E as in this case","neglect of cross‑doctor diagnosis consideration",""],[3862,1476,"most section 136 usage is out of hours when there is less resource to respond from psychiatric services","inadequate out‑of‑hours psychiatric response",""],[3863,1476,"use of film over glass in areas where patients are at increased risk of smashing windows should be more widespread in the NHSE estate","inadequate window safety in high‑risk areas",""],[3884,1482,"serious difficulty in the recording, communication, and use of ACCT documentation","inaccurate ACCT documentation",""],[3885,1482,"no formal policy then in place governing paracetamol overdose","unclear overdose management framework",""],[3886,1482,"No prison intelligence report was raised after the first reported overdose","uninvestigated source of paracetamol",""],[3887,1482,"debt to other prisoners connected with vapes","unmanaged prisoner debt",""],[3888,1482,"serious staffing shortages, recruitment and retention difficulties","operational pressures compromising safeguarding",""],[3943,1504,"The deceased took her own life on 11 January 2016 in a single occupancy cell in C1, the mental health assessment unit, of HMP Holloway, North London. She strangled herself with a tight ligature made from bed linen.","untimely fitness to plead report preparation",""],[3944,1504,"The jury concluded that the failure to conclude the fitness to plead assessment process in a sufficiently timely manner contributed to Sarah's death.","untimely fitness to plead assessment",""],[3945,1504,"The jury concluded that the decision to reduce the frequency of observations on Sarah Reed at ACCT Review No.4 on 5 January 2016, six days before Sarah's death, was inappropriate given the clear evidence of the deterioration of her mental state.","inappropriate observation frequency reduction",""],[3946,1504,"The jury also found that not all members of the ACCT Review team fully reviewed the ACCT document before making a decision.","incomplete ACCT document review",""],[3947,1504,"The jury also found the system of some members of the multi-disciplinary team recording observations which were not accessible to all other members of the team to be 'detrimental'. For example, many helpful observations about Sarah's behaviour were recorded in the prison medical notes on SystemOne by doctors and nurses, but they were not accessible to prison officers.","inaccessible observation records",""],[3948,1504,"The jury concluded that the delay in holding a Care Programme Approach (CPA) meeting was unacceptable. The evidence showed that a CPA Meeting for assessing a prisoner's long-term care should have been held within four weeks from reception. In this case it was held after nine weeks.","delayed CPA meeting",""]]